  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 PM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A warm and humid day today, with afternoon showers \nand another round of showers/thunderstorms later tonight.\n\nLast night into this morning, a boundary set up over central NC, \nbisecting the region in half. Shower activity started developing \nalong this boundary as it started lifting north over Wake county \njust after noon. PWs over the region are very high (around 2-2.3 in) \nwhich will allow showers to be efficient rain producers this \nafternoon. This will combine with slow cell motions to create an \nisolated flooding threat, especially if a shower moves over an urban \nor poor drainage area. The area of any showers/thunderstorms should \nincrease over the next few hours as the boundary continues north and \noutflows from previous showers/storms provides additional areas of \nlift. \n\nLater this evening into tonight, an additional area of \nshower/thunderstorm development may be possible as a pre-frontal \ntrough moves through central NC from the west. Any rain that \ndevelops due to this should move faster than this afternoon's rain, \nleading to a lower flooding threat. \n\nIn terms of the strength of the cells today, severe weather is not \ncurrently expected. CAPE values this afternoon look to maximize \naround 1500-2000 J/kg, with low shear values. This should allow for \na few cells to get tall enough to produce lightning, but keep any \nwinds with them below severe strength. However, a few water-loaded \ndowndrafts could produce brief periods of strong gusty winds this \nafternoon. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Limited chances of a shower or storm Sunday \nafternoon and Sunday night, with the greatest chances during the \nafternoon across the Coastal Plain.\n\nA mid level short wave trough across TN and AL at midday today will \nbe located just east of the Blue Ridge on Sunday morning and then \nmove offshore by Sunday evening. The best forcing for ascent will be \neast of this feature and should be exiting our eastern areas during \nthe afternoon. Deep layer moisture will be above average areawide on \nSunday but greatest across the east. Expect Sunday to be less \nconvectively active than today with the greatest threat of a shower \nor storm across the east during the afternoon. However, a cold front \nwill trail behind the short wave trough and move across the area \nlate Sunday night and there is a limited chance or a shower or storm \nin the lingering moisture ahead of the front on Sunday night. Highs \nshould range in the upper 80s to lower 90s with lows near 70. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... A northeast flow will result in near average \ntemperatures and lower humidity on Tuesday and Wednesday that will \nfeel cooler than recent days.\n\nAn anomalously strong mid-level ridge centered in the TN Valley on \nTuesday and Wednesday will result in a northerly flow at mid levels \nacross VA and the Carolinas while a 1030 mb surface high pressure \nsystem centered over New England will produce a northeast low level \nflow across the area. The result will be near to slightly above \nseasonal temperatures that will feel notably cooler than recent \nweeks. Highs should range in the lower to mid 80s both days and \nnighttime lows in the lower to mid 60s with some upper 50s (maybe \nmid 50s) possible in the typically cooler locations. Dry weather \nwith partly cloudy skies and a noticeable northeast breeze at times \nare expected during the period. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4... The low and mid level flow will become more east \nand southeasterly late in the work week and weekend resulting in \nwarming temperatures, an increase in humidity, and an uptick in rain \nchances. \n\nThe upper-level ridge at mid levels weakens late in the week and \nbecomes more west to east oriented as a mid level weakness/upper low \norganizes near the Bahamas and shifts west into the northern Gulf. \nMeanwhile the narrow surface ridge extending southwest into VA and \nthe Carolinas weakens late in the work week as a weak coastal front \ntries to develop off the Carolina coast. The result for our area \nwill be an increase in temperatures, moisture and clouds and \ndepending on the track and evolution of the upper low and coastal \nfont, an uptick in rain chances over the weekend that could increase \nfurther early next week.",
